= Knowledge Graph Visualization: Rendering Recursive Knowledge =

== Executive Summary

This guide documents methods for visualizing BITwiki knowledge graphs, including force-directed layouts, hierarchical views, temporal animations, and epistemic filtering.

'''Purpose:''' Transform SMW query results into visual knowledge representations that surface patterns, relationships, and epistemic quality.

----
== 1. Visualization Principles

=== 1.1 Epistemic Transparency in Visualization

Visualizations must preserve epistemic information:
* '''Node color:''' Epistemic status (Axiomatic=gold, Established=green, Emerging=yellow, Hypothetical=gray, Contested=red)
* '''Node size:''' Confidence score (larger = higher confidence)
* '''Edge thickness:''' Relationship strength
* '''Edge style:''' Relationship type (solid=implements, dashed=related, dotted=contradicts)

=== 1.2 Recursive Visualization

Visualizations should show:
* '''Self-reference:''' Entities that reference themselves
* '''Cycles:''' Circular dependency chains
* '''Emergence:''' Higher-order patterns from simple rules
* '''Evolution:''' Temporal changes in the graph

=== 1.3 Interaction Patterns

'''Required Interactions:'''
* Zoom and pan
* Node selection (show details)
* Filter by epistemic status
* Filter by confidence threshold
* Highlight paths between nodes
* Expand/collapse clusters

----
== 2. Layout Algorithms

=== 2.1 Force-Directed Layout

'''Best for:''' General exploration, community detection

'''Algorithm:'''
* Nodes repel each other (Coulomb's law)
* Edges attract connected nodes (Hooke's law)
* Iterative optimization until equilibrium

'''BITwiki Adaptation:'''
* Confidence as node mass (higher confidence = larger mass)
* Epistemic status as charge (Axiomatic repels more)
* Relationship type as spring constant

'''D3.js Implementation:'''
```javascript
const simulation = d3.forceSimulation(nodes)
  .force("charge", d3.forceManyBody()
    .strength(d => -300 * d.confidence))
  .force("link", d3.forceLink(links)
    .id(d => d.id)
    .strength(d => d.relationshipStrength))
  .force("center", d3.forceCenter(width / 2, height / 2));
```

=== 2.2 Hierarchical Layout

'''Best for:''' Taxonomies, ontologies, dependency chains

'''Algorithm:'''
* Tree or DAG layout
* Parent nodes above children
* Minimize edge crossings

'''BITwiki Adaptation:'''
* Has parent concept → hierarchical edge
* Domain → top-level nodes
* Confidence → vertical position (higher = higher)

'''Implementation:'''
```javascript
const treeLayout = d3.tree()
  .size([width, height]);

const root = d3.hierarchy(data);
treeLayout(root);
```

=== 2.3 Radial Layout

'''Best for:''' Central concepts with related entities

'''Algorithm:'''
* Central node at origin
* Related nodes on concentric circles
* Distance from center = relationship depth

'''BITwiki Adaptation:'''
* Query entity at center
* Related entities by distance
* Epistemic status as angular position

=== 2.4 Temporal Layout

'''Best for:''' Evolution tracking, version history

'''Algorithm:'''
* X-axis = time
* Y-axis = confidence or quality
* Nodes positioned by creation/modification date

'''BITwiki Adaptation:'''
* Creation date → X position
* Confidence → Y position
* Epistemic status → color
* Size → citation count

